# On the Global Theory of Special Finsler Manifolds

## Abstract / Introduction

The present paper is devoted to developing a global, coordinate-free presentation of the theory of special Finsler manifolds, with the aim of unifying and extending the treatment of many of the special structures that arise frequently in the literature and applications of Finsler geometry. While the classical development of this theory has traditionally relied heavily on local coordinate computations—often obscuring the underlying geometric content and making comparisons between different special types cumbersome—the approach adopted here seeks to recast these fundamental notions in an intrinsic, basis-free language. This shift in perspective not only clarifies the geometric meaning of the various special conditions but also facilitates a more systematic study of the interrelationships among them.

To this end, we introduce global (intrinsic) definitions for a wide range of special Finsler manifolds that have played a significant role in the theory, including but not limited to:

- **Locally Minkowskian manifolds**, characterized by the local existence of coordinate systems in which the fundamental tensor depends only on the direction and not on position;
- **Berwald manifolds**, distinguished by the vanishing of the covariant derivative of certain curvature-related tensors, or equivalently by the linearity of the connection coefficients in the fiber coordinates;
- **Landsberg manifolds**, defined through the vanishing of the Landsberg tensor, a condition intermediate between the Berwald and general Finsler settings;
- **C-reducible manifolds**, in which the Cartan torsion tensor exhibits a specific decomposable structure in terms of the metric and a distinguished covector;
- **Recurrent-type manifolds**, in which particular tensor fields (such as curvature or torsion tensors) are recurrent with respect to the Finsler connection, satisfying a proportionality condition under covariant differentiation;
- **Manifolds of scalar curvature and constant curvature**, in which the curvature tensor satisfies conditions analogous to those in Riemannian geometry, expressing the curvature in terms of a scalar function or constant together with the fundamental tensor.

Beyond these core examples, the global formalism is extended to accommodate several other special types that have appeared scattered throughout the Finsler geometry literature, thereby providing a comprehensive and unified intrinsic framework.

Having established these definitions, the paper proceeds to investigate the various logical and geometric relationships that exist among the different classes of special Finsler manifolds under consideration. In particular, we examine implications and equivalences between the defining conditions, identify hierarchies among the special types (e.g., showing how certain classes are properly contained in others), and clarify the precise conditions under which one special structure reduces to or coincides with another. This comparative analysis, carried out entirely within the global framework, yields a clearer picture of the logical architecture underlying the theory of special Finsler manifolds.

A significant portion of the paper is devoted to demonstrating the effectiveness of this coordinate-free methodology by re-deriving many classical, previously known results—originally established through local coordinate computations—using purely global techniques. This serves both as validation of the intrinsic approach and as a means of presenting these results in a more conceptually transparent manner. Moreover, the global perspective adopted here proves to be not merely a reformulation of known material but a genuinely productive tool for further investigation: several new results, previously unnoted or inaccessible through local methods, are obtained as a natural consequence of this global treatment.

As a further contribution, the systematic global investigation undertaken in this paper yields, as a by-product, a number of interesting identities and structural properties satisfied by the torsion and curvature tensor fields associated with a Finsler connection. These identities, which emerge naturally from the interplay between the various special conditions studied, enrich our understanding of the tensorial structure of Finsler manifolds and may prove useful in subsequent research.

Finally, in order to bridge the global and local perspectives and to facilitate comparison with the extensive existing literature on the subject, an appendix is included in which the intrinsic definitions and results developed in the main body of the paper are explicitly related to their local coordinate counterparts. This appendix serves as a practical dictionary between the two approaches, allowing readers familiar with the classical local treatment to readily translate between the global formalism presented here and the more traditional coordinate-based definitions and computations found elsewhere in the literature.
